\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


For a $C^2$ Riemannian metric $g$ on a manifold, $\operatorname{Sc}_g$ denotes the scalar curvature, the trace of its Ricci tensor. A lower bound $\operatorname{Sc}_g\geq\sigma$, for a real number $\sigma$, holds pointwise. The source asks about generalized geometric objects that retain analogous scalar-curvature features, including possible singular spaces obtained through limits or completions. It does not fix a particular singular category, convergence notion, or list of axioms in advance.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Identify the most general classes of geometric objects displaying properties analogous to those of $C^2$ Riemannian manifolds with positive scalar curvature and, more generally, with scalar curvature bounded below by $\sigma$. \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1908.10612v6}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Find the broadest geometric setting that retains the essential features of manifolds with positive or lower-bounded scalar curvature.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 6700009 (AMR-066-0009). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Misha Gromov, Four Lectures on Scalar Curvature (2021 version). Discussion preceding Section 3.20 and Question 1, p. 204. \url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1908.10612v6}.
\end{description}
